  "rule": "hard-xp-stop",
  "expMultiplier": null,
  "notes": [
    "A Pokemon whose level >= the active cap gains 0 EXP and 0 EVs from every source.",
    "Rare Candy and Exp Candy refuse to apply at or above the cap.",
    "Evolution is NOT capped: a Pokemon that evolves at the cap level still evolves.",
    "Day-care and traded-in Pokemon have their exp gain zeroed at or above the cap too.",
    "expMultiplier scales every exp award BEFORE the cap check, so the raised curve is reachable by natural play -- see docs/research/level-curve.md.",
    "The curve terminates at 100: Cynthia's whole team is level 100 and the player can be level 100 facing her.",
    "Jupiter's Eterna Building fight (ace 36) must stay behind the Forest Badge; the pre-Gardenia cap is 27."
  ],
  "expMultiplierMode": "per-segment",
  "expMultiplierRationale": "Caps are HARD, so surplus EXP in a segment is discarded rather than carried forward - each segment must independently supply enough EXP to reach its own cap. A single global multiplier cannot express that: a flat x2.5 over-supplies segments 1-5 (which already run 1.76-2.58 surplus at 1x) and caps the player roughly four trainers into the game, leaving ~50 early battles awarding nothing. Per-segment multipliers are derived as target_ratio / measured_ratio, floored at 1.0. Segments 10-13 sit inside the Pokemon League where the only EXP source is the Elite Four member just defeated, so a shortfall there is unrecoverable; they use a 1.35 target instead of 1.15."
}